    not_duplicate_of: []  # 647f
    pr_ref:  # 647f
    description: "EVIDENCE (production entry point, 2026-09-25, dev 78dd52c779):\n  proc helper(): int = 1\n  proc exported*(): int = helper()\n  proc private(): int = helper()\nanalyze_nim emits `private -> helper` and NO edge from `exported`. All three symbols are extracted.\n\nMECHANISM: `_find_enclosing_proc_nim` names the enclosing proc via `find_child_by_type(current, \"identifier\")`. For `proc a*` tree-sitter wraps the name in an `exported_symbol` node, so there is no direct identifier child. The lookup finds no enclosing proc, and the call is dropped.\n\nPRIOR ART:\n- INV-bisom (satisfied) had the SAME root cause in the declaration processors: 0/246 exported procs extracted on nitter. That fix covered the declaration handlers but not this lookup. Its closure stands; this is the residual.\n- WI-tosum (todo_hard) lists nim among the 25 analyzers whose enclosing lookup is unverified against INV-midag. Its procedure (assert reach first) would catch this. This row is the concrete reach loss. WI-tosum still owns the containment check.\n\nSCOPE: every call made from an exported proc, which is a Nim module's public API. Not measured on the corpus yet (nitter is the known Nim repo).\n\nASKED:\n1. Name the enclosing proc through the same helper INV-bisom's fix uses, or key on position (symbols_at / symbol_declared_by), as INV-midag did elsewhere.\n2. Add a fixture with an exported proc. Assert reach, then containment.\n3. Measure calls_total before and after on nitter.\n4. grep for other `find_child_by_type(..., \"identifier\")` name reads in nim.py that could have the same gap."  # 647f
    fields: {}  # 647f
